.TH TRIG 3
.SH NAME
sin, cos, tan, asin, acos, atan, atan2 \- trigonometric functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <math.h>

double
sin(x)
double x;

double
cos(x)
double x;

double
tan(x)
double x;

double
asin(x)
double x;

double
acos(x)
double x;

double
atan(x)
double x;

double
atan2(y, x)
double y, x;
.fi
.PP
cc ... \-lm
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR sin ,
.B cos
and
.B tan
return the sine, cosine and tangent of
.IR x ,
an angle in radians. The argument is reduced by multiples of pi/2
exactly, so that the results are right for large angles too.
.PP
.B asin
returns the arc sine of
.IR x ,
in the range \-pi/2 to pi/2;
.B acos
the arc cosine, in the range 0 to pi; and
.B atan
the arc tangent, in the range \-pi/2 to pi/2.
.PP
.B atan2
returns the arc tangent of
.IR y / x ,
in the range \-pi to pi, using the signs of both arguments to find the
quadrant: the angle of the point
.RI ( x ", " y ).
.PP
The results are within one unit in the last place of the exact value;
tan and atan2 within two. <math.h> defines M_PI as pi.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
.TP 14
.BR sin ", " cos ", " tan
of an argument larger than 1.4e16 in magnitude, where not one digit of
the result could be right, return 0, with a TLOSS error.
.TP
.BR asin ", " acos
of an argument larger than 1 in magnitude return 0, with a DOMAIN
error.
.TP
.B atan2
of two zero arguments returns 0, with a DOMAIN error.
.PP
For these errors, a message is written on the standard error. A
program can handle the errors itself with
.BR matherr (3).
.SH ERRORS
errno is set to EDOM after a DOMAIN error, and to ERANGE after a TLOSS
error.
.SH SEE ALSO
sinh(3), hypot(3), matherr(3), intro(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B sin
and
.B cos
functions first appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX. Version 7 has all of
these functions.
